## POLICY FILE — HUJSON FORMAT

The tailnet policy file uses HuJSON (human JSON) — standard JSON + C-style comments + trailing commas.
Written as a single document that controls ALL access in your tailnet.

### Top-level sections

  grants       — Modern access control (PREFERRED over acls — more features, same network effect)
  acls         — Legacy network-layer access rules (still fully supported, never removed)
  ssh          — Tailscale SSH access rules
  autoApprovers — Who can bypass admin approval for subnet routes + exit nodes
  nodeAttrs    — Extra attributes for specific devices (Funnel, NextDNS, posture)
  postures     — Device posture conditions (OS version, client version, etc.)
  tagOwners    — Who can assign each tag to devices
  groups       — Named lists of users for use in policy rules
  hosts        — Named aliases for IPs / CIDR ranges
  ipsets       — Named collections of IPs, CIDRs, hosts, autogroups
  tests        — Assertions that run on every policy save (fail = policy rejected)
  sshTests     — SSH-specific assertions
  derpMap      — Custom DERP relay servers

### GRANTS (modern access control — use for all new policy)

  {
    "grants": [
      {
        "src": ["group:engineering"],
        "dst": ["tag:server"],
        "ip": ["*"]               // network layer: all ports
      },
      {
        "src": ["group:devops"],
        "dst": ["tag:prod"],
        "ip": ["22", "443"]       // network layer: specific ports
      }
    ]
  }

  Grants replace acls. One grant = one (src, dst, capabilities) tuple.
  Action is always accept (deny-by-default means only grants are needed).
  "ip" field = network-layer ports. "*" = all TCP+UDP. ICMP is implied when any IP access exists.

### ACLS (legacy — still works, never removed, but won't get new features)

  {
    "acls": [
      {
        "action": "accept",
        "src": ["group:engineering"],
        "dst": ["tag:server:*"]   // host:port format — * means all ports
      }
    ]
  }

  action is always "accept" (deny is implied for everything else).
  dst format: "host:port" or "host:portRange" or "host:port1,port2"
  proto field optional: "tcp", "udp", "icmp", or IANA number 1-255

### SOURCE / DESTINATION SELECTORS

  *                    — Any tailnet device (not external sources)
  user@domain.com      — Specific user (all their devices)
  group:name           — Group defined in groups section
  tag:name             — All devices with this tag
  100.x.y.z            — Specific Tailscale IP
  192.168.1.0/24       — CIDR subnet (for subnet router access)
  hostname             — Alias defined in hosts section
  ipset:name           — IP set defined in ipsets section
  autogroup:member     — All direct tailnet members
  autogroup:admin      — Users with Admin role
  autogroup:owner      — Tailnet Owner
  autogroup:tagged     — Any tagged device
  autogroup:internet   — Access via exit nodes to internet
  autogroup:self       — A user's own devices (for SSH rules)
  autogroup:shared     — Users who accepted a sharing invite
  autogroup:danger-all — ALL sources including non-tailnet (use with extreme caution)

### TAGS

  Tags turn a user-owned device into a service identity device.
  Tagged devices are NOT owned by any user — they use their tag as identity in ACLs.
  Once tagged, the device cannot be untagged by just anyone — only tagOwners can re-tag.

  tagOwners defines who can assign each tag:
  {
    "tagOwners": {
      "tag:server":   ["group:ops"],
      "tag:k3s-node": ["autogroup:admin"],
      "tag:fabric":   []              // shorthand for ["autogroup:admin"]
    }
  }

  Apply a tag when registering a server:
    tailscale up --advertise-tags=tag:server,tag:prod

  Apply via API:
    POST /api/v2/device/{id}/tags
    {"tags": ["tag:server", "tag:prod"]}

  ⚠ When an OAuth key assigns tags, the requested tags must EXACTLY match the key's tags
    OR each tag must be owned by one of the key's tags via tagOwners.

### AUTOGROUP: SSH

  autogroup:nonroot — any SSH user that is not root
  localpart:*@domain.com — SSH user matching the local-part of their login email

### TESTS (policy safety net)

  "tests": [
    {
      "src": "alice@example.com",
      "accept": ["tag:dev-servers:22", "tag:dev-servers:443"],
      "deny":   ["tag:prod-servers:22"]
    }
  ]

  Tests run on every policy save. If any test fails, the ENTIRE update is rejected.
  Use tests to prevent accidental regressions — especially "deny" tests for sensitive resources.

### SSH RULES

  "ssh": [
    {
      "action": "check",          // "accept" = no reauth, "check" = periodic reauth
      "src": ["group:sre"],
      "dst": ["tag:prod"],
      "users": ["ubuntu", "autogroup:nonroot"],
      "checkPeriod": "20h"        // how often to reauth (default 12h)
    }
  ]

  SSH rules require BOTH:
    1. A grant/ACL allowing port 22 from src to dst
    2. An SSH rule authorizing the SSH users

### AUTOARPROVERS

  "autoApprovers": {
    "routes": {
      "192.168.1.0/24": ["tag:subnet-router"],
      "10.0.0.0/8":     ["group:ops"]
    },
    "exitNode": ["tag:exit-node"]
  }

  Without autoApprovers, an admin must manually approve every route/exit-node advertisement.
  With autoApprovers, matching devices get auto-approved on first advertisement.
  NOTE: Updating autoApprovers does NOT retroactively approve existing unapproved routes.
  To trigger auto-approval on an existing route: remove it from the router, then re-advertise.

